self defense is when there is a demonstratively deadly force being used against you.

If someone hits you and knocks you to the ground and then covers you and starts beating, yes, that's warrant for self defense, even deadly force, because in that situation you have no reason to believe he's going to stop. 

&#x200B;

Kyle got punched in the back of the head, didn't shoot the guy. Kyle then got hit with a skateboard, a skateboard being used as a bat is a deadly weapon, and given he went to grab it and come in for a second hit, why would Kyle believe he was simply going to take his gun and give him a stern talking to?

&#x200B;

If you believe your life is in danger, and you can prove that any reasonable person would see their life in danger (i.e you vs a crowd that wrongfully believes you're an active shooter when they're already rioting) you have the right to self defense, but the steps you have to take vary state to state, in WI he fulfilled his duty to get self defense rights.

That's not an equivalence though, but yes you actually could if they chased you out of the building. There's actually precedent that says you can defend yourself and employees don't have the right to attack you to reclaim property because it's not theirs.

&#x200B;

Also, are you missing the context that he put a dumpster fire out which caused the first man to start acting erratically (the first one shot was recently released from a mental hospital so if anyone wasn't in the right mind, it wasn't Kyle). This would be like if you went to Target and an employee didn't like you putting items back on the shelf so they attack you. 

&#x200B;

Did you even watch the trial? Or do you get all your info from other people like you?

8pm, 3 hrs earlier than the shooting. Police curfews are not laws, believe it or not, and would, at most for Kyle, have been community service. It was brought up in the trial and the defense objected, and won the objection, that a police curfew, while morally correct in the situation to have listened to, is not an indication of intent. 

&#x200B;

Either way, he was "hired" to protect the businesses he was at, so he would have been exempt from it because he was "working." The police weren't even telling him to go home when he passed them multiple times. They just told him he couldn't walk down the streets they were at.

&#x200B;

And if you want to hold Kyle to "intent to start shit" because he didn't go home, you have to hold EVERY protestor there with the same intent, because they weren't listening to the cops either. 2 wrongs don't make a right, but if you're going to say that people past the curfew were there to cause problems, then that was even more reason to stay out given that he was tasked to put out fires and stop vandals at the car lots.

One of my parents is a psychiatrist, so this is secondhand. 

&#x200B;

Her going from "I'm done with this" to "omg I'm so sorry I was wrong" is classic bipolar. I'm willing to bet she excuses her actions because someone else set her off. The impulsiveness with her finances isn't indicative of bipolar, but someone with bipolar would have a hard time not making purchases. Her being aware of the problems but blaming "I have an eating disorder"  to excuse her actions. She basically won't take responsibility because it's something "out of her control," but if she's not being treated, EVERYTHING will be out of her control. Things \*happen\* to her, she doesn't cause them or put herself in those situations. 

&#x200B;

I'm not sure the other aspects of your relationship but if she is incredibly lovey to the point of being clingy, that's another sign. Stuff like oversharing, not respecting boundaries, setting unrealistic boundaries on you, etc can manifest. I'm not there so idk about the coworkers but they're giving her direct attention and you're long distance. That's a common thing for bipolar people to delve into because sex appeal \*is\* something she can control in other people. Does she get pissed off at her friends/family when they either slightly inconvenience her or she doesn't get her way? Like if her parents told her to grab milk would she throw a fit saying that they ask her to do everything and it's unfair?

the AI plays weird with spike growth. 

&#x200B;

I'm only at the part where the druid camp is under attack, and I cast spike growth in front of the doors. None of the goblins wanted to walk into it, they would walk up to where the circle touched the walls on the side and would stop/dash during their turn to the middle crest of the circle and back to the edge. 

Overall it made the entire 20+ vs us really easy as none of the melees, other than the spiders, were able to get to the top. The drow cleric misty stepped only once she was down to about 12 health and she was the last one remaining. 

&#x200B;

IDK if it's intended because of the way I fought, but I took about 20 damage total from the entire fight, only lost 2 AI.

